  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\ntoday through Sunday.\n\nA cold front will approach from the northwest later this\nafternoon into this evening before eventually pushing south of \nthe area tonight. Showers will likely reach western Maryland by\nthe afternoon, progressing southeastward during the evening and\novernight. Thunderstorms will be few, if any, despite\ntemperatures becoming 15 degrees above average for early\nOctober. A right entrance jet streak later today and a mid-level\nelongated trough could be enough for a few places to encounter\na thunderstorm or two. Otherwise, a westerly component could result\nin downslope drying. Showers could become more scattered the\nfurther east we look later today. Rainfall amounts will be\ngenerally less than a quarter inch for most locations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls across the southeastern states. Southern stream\nmoisture will likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the\nnorth, but there remains some uncertainty in the dividing line\nbetween rain and no rain. Overall, Saturday probably has a\nbetter chance of being dry, especially the further north you go \nwith strong high pressure set up to the north. \n\nA wave of low pressure will likely ride along the boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof rain spreading northward into the area. Rain that does occur\nwill likely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nThe true Fall feel sets in next week with highs and lows near to \nbelow normal. Monday will feature broad 1025-1028 mb high pressure \nbuilding east from the Upper Midwest/western Great Lakes behind a \ndeparting upper trof/secondary cold front exiting the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast. The frontal passage itself appears mostly dry with perhaps a \nlingering shower/sprinkle if any moisture is leftover. Breezy west \nto northwest winds will accompany the front with gusts 20-30 mph and \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to mid 70s (upper 50s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead Monday night into Tuesday allowing the \nwinds to lighten and skies to clear. This will yield a prime \nradiational cooling setup, especially west of the Blue Ridge where \nwidespread frost and freeze conditions are possible. Of particular \nconcern is the high elevations and sheltered valleys (cold sinks) \nwhich could drop down to around freezing. Elsewhere, expect lows in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s. Locations near the water will be in the \nlow 50s. Similar conditions are expected Tuesday night with \nwidespread mid to upper 30s west of the Blue Ridge and north/west of \nthe I-95 metro areas. These chilly conditions (concern for frost) \nare roughly 1 to 2 weeks earlier than average.\n\nOutside of the frost and freeze potential, dry conditions will \nprevail for much of the week with broad high pressure overhead. A \ncold front looks to cross the region Thursday into Friday, perhaps \nbringing a few showers to the mountains. Low pressure lurking off \nthe Southeast coast bears monitoring, but may stay southeast of the \narea. Otherwise, another large area of high pressure returns for the \nweekend with warming temperatures heading into the following \nworkweek. Highs Tuesday through Wednesday will likely be in the 60s \n(50s mountains), with lows in the 40s to near 50 (30s mountains). \nThursday and Friday, highs warm back into the upper 60s to mid 70s \n(low 60s mountains) with lows in the 40s and 50s.",
